#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "327e406f-f432-5db4-a79f-80195c02cc96" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40ba174f-6f4c-59fc-b3cb-137e1297aa50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5429" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483689} {:answer/id #uuid "554630d6-0001-5d34-98b7-ae518e793543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483721} {:answer/id #uuid "82de29c2-442b-50b3-b8a2-f2cef0536930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5678" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483833} {:answer/id #uuid "da0abab7-344b-5db3-b96e-606236a85502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6601" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484000} {:answer/id #uuid "e46f85da-0699-50b2-9c52-f46103534b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7123" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da0abab7-344b-5db3-b96e-606236a85502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6601" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484000} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa4e5556-e66f-5ee1-bddc-c98cca0920b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "95a41c31-9a32-5733-b64d-135e1d1b45d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-143502" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n=30$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}x, & 1\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.66,$ calculate $P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3).$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0606\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0594\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0582\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0735\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0721\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0708\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0885\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0869\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0853\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7088\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7123\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7157\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7422\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7454\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7486\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7734\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7764\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7794\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{45}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{128}{45})-(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{45}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5} \\\\  & =2.2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.66.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(2.2,\\frac{0.66}{30}) \\\\  & ∼N(2.2,0.022).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3) & =P(\\frac{2-2.2}{\\sqrt{0.022}}<Z<\\frac{2.3-2.2}{\\sqrt{0.022}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.35<Z<0.67) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.67)-P(Z<-1.35) \\\\  & =Φ(0.67)-Φ(-1.35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.67)=0.7486, \\\\ Φ(-1.35)=0.0885.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3) & =Φ(0.67)-Φ(-1.35) \\\\  & =0.7486-0.0885 \\\\  & =0.6601.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "327e406f-f432-5db4-a79f-80195c02cc96" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c5bf3b4-ed42-5eb0-ad5a-8ac0efb22629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0985" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061267} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d4ec58-1e94-5b26-987f-f90f2a1f6bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1170" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061268} {:answer/id #uuid "f23c9b14-6f45-5ea6-87c4-ad37be574c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061269} {:answer/id #uuid "030e07aa-4ecb-5bb4-bc80-5f5cbb2506a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0853" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061270} {:answer/id #uuid "090ff02b-aeb9-5a1d-b36c-5054afc4b296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c5bf3b4-ed42-5eb0-ad5a-8ac0efb22629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0985" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061267} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cc45ee8-b0e6-5e8f-9a87-c2295533ec34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6df49040-5dbb-5927-a6aa-8f742c1a6cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-143503" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{25}$ be a random sample of size $n=25$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.15,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}>1.6).$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the sample size is sufficiently large for the central limit theorem to be applied. A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ | $\\,0.8810\\,$ | $\\,0.8830\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ | $\\,0.9162\\,$ | $\\,0.9177\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =25,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{32}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =1.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.15.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.5,\\frac{0.15}{25}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.5,0.006).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>1.6).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>1.6) & =P(Z>\\frac{1.6-1.5}{\\sqrt{0.006}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.29)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>1.29) & =1-P(Z<1.29) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.29).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.29)=0.9015.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>1.6) & =1-Φ(1.29) \\\\  & =1-0.9015 \\\\  & =0.0985.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9722c125-2d26-5aed-ab60-2e2358a17fdc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Discrete"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "287f4c69-8bd4-5569-9333-3f219db98ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1)-Φ(-1.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519056} {:answer/id #uuid "38fb3acb-053d-552f-a825-7ddc2d36cb45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(2)-Φ(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519079} {:answer/id #uuid "728b0f84-6442-5c3f-96b2-7e64f3c56829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.5)-Φ(-1.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519168} {:answer/id #uuid "798138a1-9c96-5b5d-9bc0-6edae93977b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(2)-Φ(-0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519182} {:answer/id #uuid "f67a96ba-09f5-59df-948a-4c2d70d715d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1)-Φ(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "798138a1-9c96-5b5d-9bc0-6edae93977b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(2)-Φ(-0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519182} :answer-field/id #uuid "64ef3aa0-5b31-5f1c-8e8c-420a16ef0aa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e9c32a8-c5ef-5dca-8493-0a63cb017fc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-143512" :question/problem "| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{101}$ be a random sample of size $n=101$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $2.5$ and $3.$\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=101,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.1+2\\cdot 0.7+4\\cdot 0.1+8\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0+1.4+0.4+0.8 \\\\  & =2.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.7+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{8}^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-{2.6}^{2} \\\\  & =(0+2.8+1.6+6.4)-6.76 \\\\  & =4.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(2.6,\\frac{4.04}{101}) \\\\  & ∼N(2.6,0.04).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(2.5<\\overset{―}{X}<3).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2.5<\\overset{―}{X}<3) & ≈P(\\frac{2.5-2.6}{\\sqrt{0.04}}<Z<\\frac{3-2.6}{\\sqrt{0.04}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.5<Z<2) \\\\  & =Φ(2)-Φ(-0.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(2.5<\\overset{―}{X}<3)=Φ(2)-Φ(-0.5).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9722c125-2d26-5aed-ab60-2e2358a17fdc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Discrete"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e0e9d16-85d3-5733-86dd-b370bb650371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483617} {:answer/id #uuid "2218abf4-e023-5af3-b0eb-30e08e6ee534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483627} {:answer/id #uuid "7a9ebc6d-26da-5439-8344-0e789ccaddc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483815} {:answer/id #uuid "c7238fb6-2845-5bcb-8fb3-76e79b105b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483960} {:answer/id #uuid "d30411eb-c7c7-50c0-be48-8d31e7a05041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d30411eb-c7c7-50c0-be48-8d31e7a05041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483990} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fbc75be-8e9b-55b7-b598-a4421edd592d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7a75f10-4331-5d53-8955-dd0d91fe7458" :question/math-academy-id "q-143514" :question/problem "| $x$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $-4$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{65}$ be a random sample of size $n=65$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is less than $-4.9.$\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=65,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot 0.1+(-6)\\cdot 0.4+(-4)\\cdot 0.4+(-2)\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =-0.8-2.4-1.6-0.2 \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =((-8)^{2}\\cdot 0.1+(-6)^{2}\\cdot 0.4+(-4)^{2}\\cdot 0.4+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-(-5)^{2} \\\\  & =(6.4+14.4+6.4+0.4)-25 \\\\  & =2.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(-5,\\frac{2.6}{65}) \\\\  & ∼N(-5,0.04).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9) & ≈P(Z<\\frac{-4.9-(-5)}{\\sqrt{0.04}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.5) \\\\  & =Φ(0.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9)=Φ(0.5).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9722c125-2d26-5aed-ab60-2e2358a17fdc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Discrete"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0898778a-e9f2-5272-b446-decc5e417146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0838" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427595} {:answer/id #uuid "25c6b726-fd80-5fc4-87c6-1f8caae104bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0594" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427596} {:answer/id #uuid "8d73d5cd-e782-589d-9c6e-6fdab9305338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0681" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427605} {:answer/id #uuid "b28f985b-b01a-5b8d-9f24-7716132f4a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0606" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427608} {:answer/id #uuid "f762fc2b-ccb2-5adb-bbf3-061f3f8eede8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0708" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f762fc2b-ccb2-5adb-bbf3-061f3f8eede8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0708" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427617} :answer-field/id #uuid "31024a23-6322-5d05-b371-cb1388d4cd05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c900f37-300a-5da2-a8b6-a15d4ba96d8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143524" :question/problem "| $x$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{100}$ be a random sample of size $n=100$ from a population with the probability mass function given above.  Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is less than $7.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.0606\\,$ | $\\,0.0594\\,$ | $\\,0.0582\\,$ | $\\,0.0571\\,$ | $\\,0.0559\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0735\\,$ | $\\,0.0721\\,$ | $\\,0.0708\\,$ | $\\,0.0694\\,$ | $\\,0.0681\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0885\\,$ | $\\,0.0869\\,$ | $\\,0.0853\\,$ | $\\,0.0838\\,$ | $\\,0.0823\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=100,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.2+5\\cdot 0.4+10\\cdot 0.2+20\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0+2+2+4 \\\\  & =8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{5}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{10}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{20}^{2}\\cdot 0.2)-{8}^{2} \\\\  & =(0+10+20+80)-64 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(8,\\frac{46}{100}) \\\\  & ∼N(8,0.46).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<7).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<7) & ≈P(Z<\\frac{7-8}{\\sqrt{0.46}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-1.47) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.47).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.47)=0.0708.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<7) & =Φ(-1.47) \\\\  & =0.0708.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
